  "notes": "West leads the ♣10 against 7NT. After winning the club opening lead, South should unblock the hearts and try 2 rounds of spades.\n\nWhen West discards on the second round of spades, East must have 4 or more diamonds for the contract to have a chance. South must plan to squeeze East in spades and diamonds. One of the threats will have to be in South's hand for the squeeze to function. As North holds winners in both spades and diamonds and South has no hand entry in either of these suits, South must execute a Vienna coup.\n\nA Vienna coup is an unblocking play to prepare for a squeeze. If South fails to cash North's winners in one of the threat suits, the last round of clubs will squeeze North before East.\n\nAssume dummy's third spade honour is cashed, establishing South's spade as an unblocked threat against East. Dummy's heart winner is cashed for a diamond pitch. East will be squeezed on the run of the clubs.\n\nOn the last club, North can spare a spade while East is squeezed. A spade discard will establish South's spade. A diamond discard will make North's third diamond a winner.",
